---
import Footer from "../../components/shared/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../../components/shared/Header.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/shared/PageHeader.astro";
import P2PSubNav from "../../components/shared/P2PSubNav.astro";
import ContactBanner from "../../components/shared/ContactBanner.astro";
import LeadBlock from "../../components/shared/LeadBlock.astro";
import P2PNetworkDiagram from "../../components/p2p/P2PNetworkDiagram.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="P2P Media Loader Case Study: P2P Video R&D | Novage"
  canonical="case-studies/p2p-media-loader"
  description="How Novage built and keeps evolving P2P Media Loader, an open-source P2P video engine served nearly 200 million times a month: architecture and results."
>
  <Header />
  <div id="content">
    <article>
      <PageHeader
        eyebrow="Case study"
        title="P2P Media Loader"
        accent="Eight years of P2P video R&D"
      >
        <p class="lead">
          How we built — and keep rebuilding — an open-source engine that turns
          viewers into a delivery network, served{" "}
          <strong>nearly 200 million times a month from jsDelivr alone</strong>.
        </p>
        <P2PSubNav current="case-study" />
      </PageHeader>

      <div class="page container content">
        <ul class="key-facts">
          <li>
            <strong>Since 2018</strong> 35 releases, 5 major versions
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>~200M</strong> monthly requests on jsDelivr
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>1,700+</strong> GitHub stars, 345 forks
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>99%</strong> of live traffic over P2P with 100 viewers,
            ideally
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The challenge</h2>
        <p>
          With a CDN, every new viewer adds to the bill. Peer-to-peer delivery
          turns that around: viewers watching the same stream share video
          segments with each other, so a bigger audience costs less per viewer,
          not more.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our aim goes further: bring the cost of delivery close to zero, and
          bring closer an era where anyone can stream 8K video to a large
          audience.
        </p>
        <p>Doing that in a browser is hard:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            no plugins — only WebRTC, HTML5 video and what each browser allows
          </li>
          <li>many video players, each seeing the same stream differently</li>
          <li>
            live streams that leave only seconds to share each new segment
          </li>
          <li>
            and one rule above all: P2P must never make playback worse than the
            CDN alone
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          On top of that, the ground keeps moving: players, browsers, streaming
          formats and mobile platforms change every year.
        </p>

        <h2>An architecture that keeps evolving</h2>
        <p>
          P2P Media Loader has never been finished. We keep reworking its
          architecture to raise the P2P ratio and to adapt to how the video
          ecosystem changes:
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Version</th>
              <th>What changed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>0.x (2018–2019)</td>
              <td>
                First versions prove P2P video in the browser for Hls.js and
                Shaka Player, with WebRTC and WebTorrent signaling
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>1.0 (2024)</td>
              <td>
                Full rewrite of the code and algorithms: iOS support, ES
                modules, P2P for split audio and video, zero configuration
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2.0 (2024)</td>
              <td>
                Pluggable segment storage — for example, IndexedDB keeps VOD
                segments across page reloads — and memory limits adapted to each
                browser
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>3.0 (2026)</td>
              <td>
                Our own browser-native WebTorrent client with no Node.js
                polyfills, active peer churning, and builds for Smart TVs and
                older browsers
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>4.0 (2026)</td>
              <td>
                Stream identity owned by the core, and predictable swarm IDs so
                private trackers can allowlist swarms
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>5.0 (2026)</td>
              <td>
                A manifest-driven core, dash.js and Video.js integrations,
                rendezvous hashing for segment ownership, and DRM support
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Key engineering decisions</h2>

        <h3>One core, thin engine integrations</h3>
        <p>
          Video engines such as HLS.js, Shaka Player, dash.js and Video.js 8
          (VHS) play segmented video through Media Source Extensions, or Managed
          Media Source on iOS. P2P Media Loader plugs into these engines and
          delivers the segments over P2P, so every player built on them gets P2P
          too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Everything the engines have in common lives in one core: P2P
          networking over WebRTC, the WebTorrent client, manifest parsing,
          segment storage and the algorithms that decide what to load from peers
          or the CDN. Each engine integration is a small package that uses the
          core through its public API. That is why integrations with HLS.js,
          Shaka Player, dash.js and Video.js 8 came together smoothly, why
          adding a new engine is straightforward, and why the same core can
          power our native mobile library.
        </p>

        <h3>Hybrid by design</h3>
        <p>
          Playback always starts over HTTP from the CDN, and the CDN stays the
          fallback for anything peers can't deliver in time. P2P fills the
          buffer ahead of the player, so a slow peer doesn't stall playback.
        </p>

        <h3>One swarm, whatever the player</h3>
        <p>
          Since version 5.0 the core parses HLS and MPEG-DASH manifests itself
          and derives each segment's identity from the manifest. Viewers on
          HLS.js, Shaka Player, dash.js or Video.js recognize the same segments
          and share them in one swarm.
        </p>

        <h3>Rendezvous hashing applied to P2P</h3>
        <p>
          When a new live segment appears, one peer should fetch it from the CDN
          and the rest should get it over P2P. We apply{" "}
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rendezvous_hashing">
            rendezvous hashing
          </a>{" "}
          among each peer's connected neighbors, not the whole swarm: every peer
          scores itself and its neighbors, and the lowest score fetches. It
          needs no coordination round trip, ownership rotates from segment to
          segment, and a late owner is covered by the others before playback is
          at risk.
        </p>
        <figure class="diagram-figure">
          <P2PNetworkDiagram />
          <figcaption>
            Each browser fetches a different segment from the CDN and shares it
            with the others, so every segment crosses the CDN once.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>Measured, not assumed</h3>
        <p>
          Changes are verified in real browsers against live and on-demand
          streams, and we measure origin traffic instead of assuming it. Placing
          the player deeper in the live window, for example, gives peers time to
          trade segments before the player asks for them, and measurably cuts
          what the origin has to deliver.
        </p>

        <h3>Safe with DRM</h3>
        <p>
          Peers exchange segments exactly as the CDN delivers them, still
          encrypted. Decryption stays in the player and license requests go
          straight to the license server, so Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay and
          HLS AES-128 streams can use P2P without weakening protection.
        </p>

        <h3>Runs everywhere</h3>
        <p>
          ES modules for modern apps and IIFE builds for Smart TVs and older
          browsers; memory limits that adapt to each browser; and engine
          integrations maintained side by side as each engine evolves.
        </p>

        <h2>The results</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Nearly 200 million requests a month</strong> on the jsDelivr
            CDN alone
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>1,700+ GitHub stars and 345 forks</strong>, and 35 releases
            since 2018
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The bigger the audience, the higher the P2P share</strong> —
            in ideal conditions, 90% of live traffic goes over P2P with 10
            viewers, 99% with 100 and 99.9% with 1,000
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>
              Integrations with HLS.js, Shaka Player, dash.js and Video.js 8
              (VHS)
            </strong>{" "}
            — all built on one shared core, so every player on these engines
            gets P2P
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A complete open-source stack</strong>, together with our{" "}
            <a href="/case-studies/wt-tracker">wt-tracker</a> for self-hosted
            signaling
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What's next: native mobile apps</h2>
        <p>
          Today P2P Media Loader runs in mobile browsers and can be{" "}
          <a href="/blog/integrate-p2p-video-streaming-into-mobile-application">
            embedded into native apps
          </a>
          . Our current R&amp;D goes further: a Kotlin Native library that
          builds for both iOS and Android.
        </p>
        <p>
          It runs a local HTTP proxy built on Kotlin Native networking APIs. Any
          player in the app sends its media requests to the proxy, which
          forwards them to a hidden WebView running the same P2P Media Loader
          core as on the web. The WebView provides the WebRTC engine and the
          runtime for our JavaScript algorithms, so apps don't have to ship
          their own WebRTC implementation. The library is at the prototype
          stage.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
          P2P Media Loader is the kind of work we do best: turning a hard
          networking problem into a product people rely on, then improving it
          release after release as the platforms around it change. We bring the
          same approach to client projects — new algorithms, performance work on
          systems in production, and R&amp;D on problems without an
          off-the-shelf answer.
        </p>

        <LeadBlock title="Have a hard problem in video or networking?">
          The team behind P2P Media Loader integrates it into your players and
          apps, builds custom P2P CDNs, and supports them in production. We also
          take on the complete development cycle for other products — product
          design, development, QA, and delivery.
        </LeadBlock>
      </div>
    </article>
    <ContactBanner />
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
